const EN = `# Travel Types in Vietnam

Vietnam is a long, narrow country. Every region has a different style, so there is a trip for almost every kind of traveler. Use this guide to find the style that fits you.

---

## Culture and History Lovers
Vietnam has a very long history, and it is easy to learn about it in the cities.
- In Hanoi, walk through the Old Quarter, visit the Temple of Literature, and see Hoa Lo Prison.
- In Ho Chi Minh City, visit the War Remnants Museum and the Reunification Palace.
- Take a day trip to the Cu Chi Tunnels to see how people lived and moved underground.
- Go with a local guide when you can. A guide helps you understand what you see.

---

## Food Lovers
Food is one of the best reasons to visit Vietnam.
- Try pho, banh mi, fresh spring rolls, and bun cha. Each region has its own special dishes.
- Street food is part of daily life. Choose busy stalls where many local people eat.
- Take a food tour or a cooking class. It is a good way to learn what you are eating.
- Vietnamese coffee is strong and sweet. Try egg coffee in Hanoi and iced coffee with milk in Ho Chi Minh City.

---

## Nature and Adventure
Vietnam has mountains, rivers, caves, and green rice fields.
- Ninh Binh, near Hanoi, has quiet rivers and tall limestone hills. Many people visit on a day trip.
- Ha Long Bay is famous for its rocks and calm water. Join a day trip or an overnight cruise.
- Sapa, in the north, is a popular place for hiking and mountain views.
- Check the weather and use a trusted tour company for boats, hiking, and other outdoor activities.

---

## Beach and Island Travelers
Vietnam has a long coast with many beaches.
- Phu Quoc is a popular island for sun and sea.
- Da Nang, Nha Trang, and Hoi An are also well known for beach time.
- Beach weather is different in each region, so choose your dates with care.

---

## City Break Travelers
If you only have a few days, the two big cities are a good choice.
- Hanoi is calmer, older, and full of lakes, temples, and small streets.
- Ho Chi Minh City is faster and more modern, with big markets, cafes, and nightlife.
- Many travelers visit both cities. A short domestic flight connects them.

---

## Backpackers and Budget Travelers
Vietnam is friendly for travelers who want to spend less.
- Hostels, local food, and public transport help you save money.
- Many travelers move between cities by train or bus.
- Keep some cash for small shops and street food stalls.

---

## Families with Children
Vietnam is a warm and welcoming country for families.
- Choose a slow plan with fewer stops. Hot weather and long travel days can be tiring.
- Water puppet shows in Hanoi, boat rides, and beach days are often popular with children.
- Take care with street food, and always carry water.
- Traffic is busy. Hold children's hands when you cross the road.

---

## Couples and Honeymoon Travelers
- Quiet beach resorts, river cruises, and old towns are popular for couples.
- Hoi An is known for its lantern streets in the evening.
- Book rooms early for holidays and busy months.

---

## Solo Travelers
- Vietnam is a popular country for solo travel, and it is easy to meet other travelers.
- Stay in well-reviewed hotels or hostels in central areas.
- Share your plan with a friend or family member, and keep copies of your documents.

---

## Luxury Travelers
- Big cities and beach areas have high-end hotels, spas, and fine dining.
- Private tours and private drivers give you more comfort and flexibility.

---

## When Is the Best Time to Go?
Vietnam has different weather in the north, center, and south.
- **North (Hanoi):** Autumn and spring are usually pleasant. Winter can be cool. Summer is hot and wet.
- **South (Ho Chi Minh City):** It is usually warm all year. The dry season is often more comfortable for sightseeing. The rainy season brings short, heavy rain.
- **Center:** Storms and heavy rain are more common in the later part of the year.

Weather can change from year to year. Check a weather forecast before you book.

---

## Simple Planning Tips
- Choose one or two travel styles, not all of them.
- Do not plan too many cities in a short time. Distances are long.
- Leave some free time. Many good memories come from small, unplanned moments.
- Check visa rules before you travel. See the Visa Info guide in this menu.`

